.48047 8L3.48047 8L3.48047 0L4.48047 6.5567e-08Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003C\/svg>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_1f29e87b_0_1_YU",{"__html":"\u003Csvg class=\"_8-pl\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" viewBox=\"0 0 8 8\" fill=\"#677B8C\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\">\u003Cpath d=\"M0 3.51996H8V4.51996H0V3.51996Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003Cpath d=\"M4.48047 6.5567e-08L4.48047 8L3.48047 8L3.48047 0L4.48047 6.5567e-08Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003C\/svg>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_1f29e87b_0_2_Ke",{"__html":"\u003Csvg class=\"_8-pl\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" viewBox=\"0 0 8 8\" fill=\"#677B8C\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\">\u003Cpath d=\"M0 3.51996H8V4.51996H0V3.51996Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003Cpath d=\"M4.48047 6.5567e-08L4.48047 8L3.48047 8L3.48047 0L4.48047 6.5567e-08Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003C\/svg>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_1f29e87b_0_3_Hq",{"__html":"\u003Csvg class=\"_8-pl\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" viewBox=\"0 0 8 8\" fill=\"#677B8C\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\">\u003Cpath d=\"M0 3.51996H8V4.51996H0V3.51996Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003Cpath d=\"M4.48047 6.5567e-08L4.48047 8L3.48047 8L3.48047 0L4.48047 6.5567e-08Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003C\/svg>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_1f29e87b_0_4_LB",{"__html":"\u003Csvg class=\"_8-pl\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" viewBox=\"0 0 8 8\" fill=\"#677B8C\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\">\u003Cpath d=\"M0 3.51996H8V4.51996H0V3.51996Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003Cpath d=\"M4.48047 6.5567e-08L4.48047 8L3.48047 8L3.48047 0L4.48047 6.5567e-08Z\" fill=\"#677B8C\">\u003C\/path>\u003C\/svg>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_0_2A",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Retrieval-augmented language models increasingly use multi-step search to solve knowledge-intensive tasks, yet existing systems typically expose retrieval through fixed pipelines or collections of atomic tools. We introduce Retrieval Program Learning (RPL), a programmable search framework that allows a policy to compose search primitives into executable programs with local state and control flow. This enables adaptive search that can retrieve broadly, process intermediate results, and selectively construct model context without tying retrieval breadth to context consumption. However, the resulting flexibility creates a difficult exploration problem: useful programs may require several coordinated decisions, while reinforcement learning often supplies only sparse feedback. To address this challenge, we propose program mutation, a grammar-constrained and execution-verified procedure that generates local variants of suboptimal programs sampled during RL, converts them into training trajectories, and distills them back into the policy. We evaluate RPL on open-domain question answering and corpus-level reasoning tasks, comparing it with a range of retrieval and reasoning baselines. RPL consistently improves open-domain QA performance across model sizes and in- and out-of-domain benchmarks, with particularly large gains on multi-hop tasks. Notably, a 4B model trained with RPL outperforms all 8B model baselines. On corpus-level reasoning, RPL achieves the highest answer F1 and document-coverage F1 for both model sizes while substantially reducing context consumption and retrieval turns. Our results show that programmable search expands the retrieval strategies that agents can learn, while program mutation provides an effective mechanism for exploring useful program structures beyond direct on-policy sampling.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_1_XZ",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We introduce Muse Glimmer, an open-source 30B parameter causal language model with perception encoder, purpose-built for autonomous agentic tasks on consumer hardware. Muse Glimmer integrates multi-step reasoning, reliable tool use and failure recovery into a single model that runs locally on consumer devices without requiring cloud infrastructure. The model accepts text and image inputs and produces text outputs, was trained on data from over a hundred languages, and is quantized to\napproximately 4-bit precision for efficient single-GPU inference. Muse Glimmer achieves state-of-the-art performance for its size class on agentic benchmarks, setting a new standard compared to the models of its size: Gemma4-31B and Qwen3.6-27B on tool use, multi-step planning, and agentic task completion. We release the model under the Apache 2.0 license to foster open-source development of capable, safe, local AI agents.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_2_Sc",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">PURPOSE: To demonstrate an end-to-end research workflow combining a large electronic health record (EHR) resource and a multi-agent artificial intelligence (AI) system governed by Human-Guided Orchestration (HGO), using sequential sacituzumab govitecan (SG) and trastuzumab deruxtecan (T-DXd) orders in breast cancer as the clinical use case.\n\nMETHODS: A June 2026 EHR release contained 4.95 million patients, including 156,051 with breast cancer codes. Muse Spark supported cohort construction, executable statistical analysis, data-quality checks, literature retrieval, and reporting executed through Symbiotic Agent Teaming (SAT) under investigator direction. The main clinical measure was the second-drug record span, from first to last order. Analyses were descriptive and unadjusted; orders did not confirm administration or progression.\n\nRESULTS: The workflow identified 106 patients: 73 had T-DXd ordered first and 33 had SG ordered first. Median second-drug spans were 63 days (interquartile range [IQR], 29\u2013116) for SG after T-DXd and 49 days (IQR, 21\u2013126) for T-DXd after SG; the median difference was \u221214 days (95\u0025 bootstrap CI, \u221241 to 21; distributional P = 0.42). Reported outputs additionally included cohort definitions, aggregate analyses, evidence context, figures, and a preliminary draft manuscript.\n\nCONCLUSION: This study provides a concrete model for integrating large EHR resources, agentic AI, and human expertise across the research cycle. The ADC use case illustrates how computational execution and Expert-in-the-Loop (EITL) adjudication can produce interpretable research outputs and refine their scientific meaning, providing a foundation for extending and evaluating this approach across clinical questions.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_3_0d",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">OBJECTIVE: To describe (1) US emergency department-treated pickleball injury burden and the injury-to-participation measure among total players, with burden among adults aged 60 to 79 years as a descriptive subgroup, and (2) determine whether general internet search interest for &quot;pickleball&quot; is also associated with total pickleball-related injuries.\n\nDESIGN: Ecological time series study, United States, 2014 to 2023.\n\nDATA SOURCES: National Electronic Injury Surveillance System (NEISS) emergency department microdata yielded 1,738 adjudicated pickleball cases, 921 of them orthopedic, weighted to an estimated 101,450 injuries nationally. Google Trends, English Wikipedia pageviews, and GDELT news volume served as general pickleball attention signals, with the Trends index for &quot;sourdough&quot; as a negative control. Sports and Fitness Industry Association (SFIA) participation estimates supplied a total player denominator.\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Annual national injury estimates with design-based 95 percent confidence intervals; injury-to-participation measures; and correlations between total pickleball-related injuries and general pickleball attention signals before and after removing trend and seasonality.\n\nRESULTS: Estimated emergency department-treated pickleball injuries rose 17.5-fold, from 1,391 in 2014 (highly unstable) to 24,294 in 2023. 70 percent of adjudicated cases involved adults aged 60 to 79. During 2017-2023, injury estimates rose 4.0-fold and participation rose about 4.4-fold. No temporal trend was detected in the injury-to-participation measure (P=0.59). Search interest looked like an excellent proxy: in annual levels it correlated with total pickleball-related injuries at R=0.96, and Pickleball Wikipedia pageviews at R=0.98. However, so did searches for &quot;sourdough&quot;, at R=0.76. Removing trend and seasonality left little (monthly residual R=0.084; year-on-year R=0.28 over 96 months, 2015-2023), and the seasons ran opposite, with injuries peaking in February and searches in July, such that, on 12-month mean profiles, the sourdough negative control fitted the injury season (R=0.96) while pickleball ran against it (R=-0.40). General pickleball search interest and pickleball news coverage remained correlated in year-on-year changes in 2017-2023 (R=0.64), whereas their corresponding correlations with total pickleball-related injuries in 2017-2023 were 0.25 and 0.20.\n\nCONCLUSIONS: The absolute burden of emergency department-treated pickleball injury has grown substantially, but the available surveillance data cannot resolve whether the sport has become more hazardous per participant. The observed rise is consistent with growth in exposure alone. The strong correlation between search interest and injury counts was not specific, the association was substantially attenuated after detrending, and the seasonal patterns were opposed. These descriptive results do not establish that search interest can substitute for injury surveillance, and series that rise together should not be assumed to be related.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_4_8T",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Importance: Infective endocarditis (IE) caused by Staphylococcus aureus remains associated with high early mortality. Staphylococcus lugdunensis is a virulent coagulase-negative species, but endocarditis-specific comparisons remain limited, and prior comparisons pooled methicillin-susceptible and methicillin-resistant S aureus.\n\nObjective: To compare 30-day mortality and major complications across methicillin-susceptible S aureus (MSSA), methicillin-resistant S aureus (MRSA), and S lugdunensis infective endocarditis.\n\nDesign, Setting, and Participants: This cohort study used the Protege database frozen June 4, 2026 (4,950,000 patients). Adults with a first coded IE episode and organism evidence within 30 days formed 3 mutually exclusive groups. Patients with mixed organism evidence were excluded from the primary analysis. The analytic cohort included 4,388 adults: 290 with S lugdunensis, 2,338 with MSSA, and 1,760 with MRSA endocarditis.\n\nExposure: Infective endocarditis organism group: MSSA, MRSA, or S lugdunensis.\n\nMain Outcomes and Measures: The primary outcome was death within 30 days after the index date. Secondary outcomes within 30 days included valve surgery, septic embolism, stroke, sepsis, heart failure, acute kidney injury, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, and epidural abscess.\n\nResults: Patients with S lugdunensis were older (mean age, 58.3 years vs 53.3 years for MSSA and 50.7 years for MRSA). Crude 30-day mortality was 5.4\u0025 (15 of 280) with S lugdunensis, 7.2\u0025 (164 of 2,292) with MSSA, and 7.4\u0025 (128 of 1,719) with MRSA. After overlap weighting, MRSA had higher 30-day mortality than S lugdunensis (8.7\u0025 vs 5.4\u0025, risk ratio, 1.63 [95\u0025 CI, 1.02-3.47]). MSSA mortality was also higher (7.9\u0025, risk ratio, 1.47 [95\u0025 CI, 0.99-2.82]). Overlap-weighted HRs were 1.49 (95\u0025 CI, 0.86-2.58) and 1.67 (95\u0025 CI, 0.95-2.92). Valve surgery within 30 days was more frequent with S lugdunensis (weighted risk, 7.7\u0025 vs 5.3\u0025 for MSSA and 3.6\u0025 for MRSA). Septic embolism and osteoarticular coded outcomes were more frequent in both S aureus groups.\n\nConclusions and Relevance: MRSA and MSSA infective endocarditis had higher 30-day mortality than S lugdunensis infective endocarditis after overlap weighting. Valve surgery was more frequent with S lugdunensis, and embolic and osteoarticular outcomes were more frequent with S aureus. Organism and methicillin susceptibility should be reported separately in endocarditis outcome studies.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_5_wh",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Health systems increasingly record ICD-10 Z59 social-needs codes for housing, food, transportation, and related hardship to trigger referrals, report quality, and adjust risk. However code documentation occurs at varying rates and no study has separated what the code says about a patient&#039;s hardship from what it says about being documented at all. Using a linked electronic health record and claims corpus covering 4.95 million patients in 21 identifiable health systems, we matched 26,153 adults with a first outpatient Z59 code to similar uncoded patients and, as a negative control, repeated the entire design around an administrative code. Coding ranged from 1 percent to 24 percent of patients across systems and remained highly variable after adjustment for patient mix. A hardship code was associated with 67\u0025 (95\u0025 CI=1.61, 1.74) more emergency department (ED) visits and 43\u0025 ( 95\u0025 CI=1.38, 1.49) more hospital admissions in the next year (62 versus 49 of every 100 patients with any acute event). The administrative-code reproduced 46 percent of the Z59 association for ED visits and 70 percent for admissions. In the five systems with the most complete stay dates, no hardship-specific admission excess was detectable. Homelessness codes forecast two and a half times the ED visit rate. At national planning prices, excess emergency and preventable hospital care totaled roughly $2,000\u2013$2,500 per coded patient-year ($2,516 base scenario; about $1,100 under an exploratory assumed-discount scenario (not an estimate of hardship- attributable spending)), with the largest emergency burden among Medicaid patients. The Z code added no increment to clinical and utilization-based risk prediction models in any\nsystem.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_6_X7",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">The MMWR reported on October 2, 2026 that Cladophialophora bantiana identification had increased in recent years at one reference laboratory and that more data were needed to identify risk factors. This analysis of a 4.95M patient electronic health record using the Meta Muse AI platform identified 21 patients with confirmed or probable C. bantiana identified in brain (7) or lung (14) samples. Among the seven brain infection patients, only two died. Artificial intelligence can greatly increase the speed of the analysis of large datasets and make these analyses accessible to more researchers.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_7_fN",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Importance: Electric scooters (e-scooters) and electric bicycles (e-bikes) have become increasingly common in US cities since 2017. However, national estimates of emergency department (ED) visits and severity trends using evaluated case definitions remain limited.\n\nObjective: To estimate national trends in ED visits, severity shares, and patient characteristics for e-scooter and e-bike injuries from 2014 to 2023, with conventional-bicycle injuries as a comparison series.\n\nDesign, Setting, and Participants: Retrospective observational study of the National Electronic Injury Surveillance System (NEISS), a stratified probability sample of US hospital EDs, 2014\u20132023. Cases were ED visits for injuries involving a ridden e-scooter, e-bike, or conventional bicycle, with no age restriction.\n\nExposures: Calendar year and device type.\n\nMain Outcomes and Measures: Trends in annual national estimates of ED visit counts, severity shares (admission, transfer, fracture), and patient characteristics.\n\nResults: The analysis included 5,514 e-scooter and 1,732 e-bike cases, representing an estimated 186,361 and 74,646 ED visits nationally from 2014 to 2023. From 2019 to 2023, estimated e-scooter visits increased from 14,714 to 50,209 (3.4-fold; 95\u0025 CI, 1.9-4.9), and e-bike visits increased from 2,076 (unstable 2019 baseline; n = 52; coefficient of variation, 36.7\u0025) to 31,517 (absolute increase, 29,442; 95\u0025 CI, 18,246-40,638; 2022-2023 ratio, 1.39; 95\u0025 Fieller CI, 1.12-2.03). The proportion of e-scooter cases involving patients younger than 18 years decreased from 50.9\u0025 (95\u0025 CI, 36.0\u0025-65.9\u0025) in 2014-2018 to 19.4\u0025 (95\u0025 CI, 13.8\u0025-25.0\u0025) in 2019-2023. From 2019 to 2023, admitted-or-transferred proportions ranged from 10.9\u0025 to 15.4\u0025 (e-scooter) and from 7.4\u0025 to 14.9\u0025 (e-bike), and primary-diagnosis fracture proportions ranged from 27.1\u0025 to 30.2\u0025 and from 17.4\u0025 to 29.0\u0025, respectively, with no statistically detectable annual trend. Over the decade, an estimated 31,640 visits resulted in admission or transfer. In contrast, conventional-bicycle visits decreased 23\u0025 from 2014 to 2023 (ratio, 0.77; 95\u0025 CI, 0.61-0.93). Consumer Product Safety Commission surveillance reported 381 e-scooter and e-bike deaths from 2017 to 2023.\n\nConclusions and Relevance: In this retrospective observational study, US ED visits involving e-scooters and e-bikes increased substantially from 2019 to 2023, while conventional-bicycle visits declined from 2014 to 2023. No trend in admission-or-transfer or fracture proportions was detected from 2019 to 2023, although estimates were imprecise and do not establish that severity was unchanged. The scale of this growth represents a substantial and ongoing demand on emergency and inpatient care.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_8_mS",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Dental and odontogenic infections cause transient oral bacteremia. Whether antecedent dental disease identifies a distinct bloodstream infection (BSI) phenotype is unknown. In multicenter BSI-PATH-v1 (19,834 pathogen-containing episodes from 17,686 patients), we compared five antecedent dental phenotypes (days 1\u201390) against oral-organism BSI (2,170 episodes, 10.9\u0025) using case-case logistic regression with prespecified confounders and Holm correction. For oral-organism BSI, adjusted odds ratios were 3.22 (1.88\u20135.49) for periodontal disease, 3.53 (2.38\u20135.24) for apical\/pulpal disease, 5.11 (2.29\u201311.42) for odontogenic abscess or cellulitis, and 3.91 (2.62\u20135.84) for composite dental disease. Associations peaked within 7 days and were null by days 31\u201390. Dental procedures (0.92, 0.57\u20131.47) showed no association. Post-BSI enrichment suggests organism-aware ascertainment. On a locked test set (n=2,966), the clinical-only model reached AUROC 0.686 and AUPRC 0.247. Dental features added no incremental value ( AUROC \u22120.0005;  AUPRC \u22120.017). Antecedent odontogenic disease identifies a distinct BSI phenotype but does not improve pathogen prediction.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_9_3o",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Importance: Arrhythmias and conduction disorders are recognized complications of acute infective endocarditis (IE), but the timing of new diagnoses after the acute episode is poorly characterized.\n\nObjective: To characterize the timing of incident arrhythmia and conduction disorder diagnoses after a first IE episode during 5 years of follow-up.\n\nDesign, Setting, and Participants: In this cohort study of the Protege database, adults with a first IE diagnosis were matched to encounter-indexed bacteremia controls without IE by sex and 10-year age strata (up to 5:1). Of 12,553 eligible patients with IE, 9,599 were matched to 47,974 controls. After washout, 6,810 patients with IE and 37,463 controls remained. Models adjusted for prespecified baseline factors.\n\nExposure: Infective endocarditis.\n\nMain Outcomes and Measures: The primary outcome was incident composite diagnosis of atrial fibrillation or flutter, supraventricular tachycardia, ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, sick sinus syndrome, and atrioventricular block. The primary estimand was the days 31-365 HR among patients outcome-free through day 30. Analyses also addressed acute and annual intervals, landmarks, absolute risks, competing risk, overlap weighting, population controls, and a cataract control.\n\nResults: Of 6,810 patients with IE, 805 (11.8\u0025) had an index-date composite diagnosis vs 1,829 of 37,463 controls (4.9\u0025). Same-day diagnoses were reported separately. Among patients outcomefree through day 30, the days 31-365 HR was 1.73 (95\u0025 CI, 1.53-1.96, 383 vs 1,150 events), 2.41 (95\u0025 CI, 2.00-2.92) for days 31-90 and 1.36 (95\u0025 CI, 1.15-1.61) for days 91-365. Standardized 1-year risks were 6.98\u0025 after IE and 4.14\u0025 after bacteremia (difference, 2.84 percentage points). The Fine-Gray HR was 1.72 (95\u0025 CI, 1.52-1.95).\n\nConclusions and Relevance: Among patients outcome-free through day 30, IE was associated with a higher rate of incident arrhythmia and conduction disorder diagnoses during days 31-365 than bacteremia without IE, with the largest association during days 31-90. The 1-year absolute difference was modest. The findings do not establish causality or support routine extended monitoring for all patients. They support prospective evaluation of focused assessment during months 2 to 3 after IE (days 31 to 90).\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_a_HI",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Objective: To estimate how often remission of type 2 diabetes is recorded in routine care, how long it lasts, and what ends it, separating a return of hyperglycemia from a restart of treatment.\n\nResearch design and methods: We studied 136,781 adults aged 18 years or older with laboratory-confirmed type 2 diabetes in the Prot\u00e9g\u00e9 linked electronic health record (EHR) and claims database. The 96,817 adults with glucose-lowering treatment recorded at or before cohort entry formed the primary (previously treated) cohort; 39,964 adults with no treatment recorded at entry were analyzed in parallel. Remission required two HbA1c values &lt;6.5\u0025, 90\u2013365 days apart, the first at least 3 months after all glucose-lowering drugs were discontinued, with no therapy recorded between them. After remission we followed glycemic relapse (HbA1c \u22656.5\u0025) and treatment restart (a new outpatient prescription or dispensed fill) separately, with death as a competing event (Aalen\u2013Johansen).\n\nResults: Remission was documented in 4.33\u0025 of previously treated adults cohort and 15.57\u0025 in the cohort of untreated adults at entry. Five-year cumulative incidence (Aalen\u2013Johansen) was 3.82\u0025 (95\u0025 CI 3.69\u20133.93) and 13.27\u0025 (12.90\u201313.59) in each cohort, respectively. Remission was less common with greater baseline treatment burden: exploratory adjusted 5-year incidence was 4.65\u0025 in adults with 1 glucose-lowering class with a treatment episode recorded at or before entry, 3.62\u0025 with two, 2.69\u0025 with three, and 2.02\u0025 with four or more such drugs.. The Median Kaplan\u2013Meier duration of first remission was 226 days (IQR 93\u2013855) in previously treated adults and 371 days (166\u20131,027) in adults untreated at entry; 37.9\u0025 (treated cohort) and 23.1\u0025 (untreated cohort) of episodes had ended by 152 days. Among remission episodes that had ended, median duration was 159 days (71\u2013362) and 265 days (129\u2013565) for the treated and untreated at entry cohort, respectively. Within 5 years of remission in previously treated adults, the first event ending remission was glycemic relapse in 31.94\u0025, treatment restart in 47.83\u0025, and death in 4.65\u0025.Treatment restart was about 1.5 times as common as glycemic relapse as the first event ending remission. A second remission followed within 5 years in 19.53\u0025 of previously treated and 24.10\u0025 of untreated remitters.\n\nConclusions: Recorded remission was uncommon and short-lived, and it ended more often with a treatment restart than with a rise in HbA1c. Studies of remission in real-world data should report relapse and restart separately and state how they confirm remission and establish that treatment stopped.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_b_am",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Importance: Patients with behavioral health presentations leave the emergency department (ED) before evaluation more often than other patients, but outcomes after leaving without being seen (LWBS) have not been described in this population.\n\nObjective: To compare return visits, admission, and mortality after LWBS vs completed care among behavioral health ED episodes.\n\nDesign, Setting, and Participants: This retrospective episode-level cohort study used a multisystem deidentified electronic health record database of US health systems (data snapshot June 4, 2026). Participants were patients of all ages with an ED visit and same-day behavioral health diagnosis. Index visits (recorded January 26, 2001 to April 13, 2025) each opened a 30-day episode window; in-window were counted as outcomes, and patients could contribute episodes to either group. Data were analyzed in October 2026.\n\nExposure: LWBS (diagnosis code Z53.21 on a date with an ED encounter) vs completed care ending in discharge home.\n\nMain Outcomes and Measures: The primary outcome was ED return visit within 7 days. Secondary outcomes were return visit within 3 and 30 days; admission on day 0, on days 1-7 (principal measure), and within 30 days; and all-cause death within 7 and 30 days, assessed among episodes from 2017 through 2023 using a verified death-record definition.\n\nResults: The analysis included 1,305,812 episodes (12,248 LWBS; 1,293,564 completed care) from 525,490 patients; 666,930 episodes (51.1\u0025) involved female patients and 66,733 (5.1\u0025) involved patients younger than 18 years. Return visit within 7 days followed 24.5\u0025 of LWBS episodes vs 13.2\u0025 of completed-care episodes (risk ratio [RR], 1.86; 95\u0025 CI, 1.81-1.92), and admission on days 1-7 followed 17.1\u0025 vs 3.9\u0025 (RR, 4.41; 95\u0025 CI, 4.24-4.59). Among 2017-2023 episodes, 30-day mortality was 0.69\u0025 (70 of 10,141) after LWBS vs 0.32\u0025 (2,426 of 756,140) after completed care (RR, 2.15; 95\u0025 CI, 1.70-2.73). Results were similar under alternative episode definitions.\n\nConclusions and Relevance: Among patients with behavioral health ED presentations, LWBS was followed by higher rates of return visits, admission, and short-term mortality than completed care. These unadjusted findings describe outcome burden, not causal effects, and highlight patients who leave as a target for rapid follow-up.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_c_e+",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We propose an energy-based model (EBM) of protein conformations that operates at atomic scale. The model is trained solely on crystallized protein data.\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_d_XV",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Contrary to most natural language processing research, which makes use of static datasets, humans learn language interactively, grounded in an environment. In this work we propose an interactive learning procedure called Mechanical\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_e_8w",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We design an online end-to-end speech recognition system based on Time-Depth Separable (TDS) convolutions and Connectionist Temporal Classification (CTC). The system has almost three times the throughput of a well tuned hybrid ASR baseline\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_f_5m",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">How do we know if communication is emerging in a multi-agent system? The vast majority of recent papers on emergent communication show that adding a communication channel leads to an increase in reward or task success. This is a useful\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_g_ei",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">The AlphaGo, AlphaGo Zero, and AlphaZero series of algorithms are remarkable demonstrations of deep reinforcement learning\u2019s capabilities, achieving superhuman performance in the complex game of Go with progressively increasing autonomy.\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_h_0e",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Research on 2D and 3D generative models typically focuses on the final artifact being created, e.g., an image or a 3D structure. Unlike 2D image generation, the generation of 3D objects in the real world is commonly constrained by the process\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_i_XA",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">This paper considers the problem of approximate nearest neighbor search in the compressed domain. We introduce polysemous codes, which offer both the distance estimation quality of product quantization and the efficient comparison of binary\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_j_On",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Modern neural networks are over-parametrized. In particular, each rectified linear hidden unit can be modified by a multiplicative factor by adjusting input and output weights, without changing the rest of the network. Inspired by the\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_k_01",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">This paper considers a learnable approach for comparing and aligning videos. Our architecture builds upon and revisits temporal match kernels within neural networks: we propose a new temporal layer that finds temporal alignments by maximizing\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_l_bl",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Continuous word representations learned separately on distinct languages can be aligned so that their words become comparable in a common space. Existing works typically solve a least-square regression problem to learn a rotation aligning a\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_m_yu",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">This paper describes Facebook AI&#039;s submission to the WAT 2019 Myanmar-English translation task. Our baseline systems are BPE-based transformer models.\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_3077fa80_0_n_Pw",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">This paper considers the problem of inferring image labels from images when only a few annotated examples are available at training time. This setup is often referred to as low-shot learning, where a standard approach is to re-train the last\u2026\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"]],"elements":[["__elem_c37bd704_0_0_1Y","u_0_0_GH",1],["__elem_c37bd704_0_1_zs","u_0_1_CM",1],["__elem_fc9f538f_0_0_eI","u_0_2_FN",1],["__elem_fc9f538f_0_1_k4","u_0_3_oT",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_k_rR","u_0_4_Na",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1n_K8","u_0_5_pd",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_l_TR","u_0_6_tC",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1o_++","u_0_7_Xx",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_m_+P","u_0_8_WZ",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1p_aw","u_0_9_RY",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_n_HQ","u_0_a_z2",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1q_vw","u_0_b_AQ",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_o_vf","u_0_c_yj",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1r_UV","u_0_d_Gr",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_p_ua","u_0_e_PJ",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1s_Bp","u_0_f_fP",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_q_ls","u_0_g_mc",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1t_wd","u_0_h_W8",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_r_e5","u_0_i_7z",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1u_Xv","u_0_j_rs",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_s_Bn","u_0_k_6y",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1v_Jw","u_0_l_B4",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_t_km","u_0_m_sE",1],["__elem_072b8e64_